  "activity": {
    "id": "dna_structure_replication",
    "name": "DNA Structure and Semiconservative Replication",
    "description": "An interactive exploration of DNA's three-dimensional double helix structure and the molecular mechanisms of semiconservative replication. Students examine the sugar-phosphate backbone, nitrogenous bases, hydrogen bonding patterns, antiparallel strand orientation, and the coordinated action of helicase, single-strand binding proteins, DNA polymerase, and DNA ligase at the replication fork. This 200-level undergraduate module builds understanding from structural components through to the asymmetric synthesis of leading and lagging strands and the production of two daughter DNA molecules each containing one parental and one newly synthesized strand.",
    "workplace": "dna_replication_lab",
    "start": "step1_dna_components",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step1_dna_components",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Explore DNA Components: Sugar-Phosphate Backbone and Nitrogenous Bases",
          "description": "Begin by examining the structural building blocks of DNA. You will see a 3D model of the DNA double helix. Identify the two sugar-phosphate backbones spiraling around a central axis, and observe how the four nitrogenous bases (adenine, thymine, guanine, cytosine) project inward toward the helix axis. Note the alternating arrangement of sugar and phosphate groups along the backbone. This module establishes the foundation for understanding how these components assemble into the characteristic double helix structure."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "dna_double_helix",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_helix_structure",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "classroom_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "replication_audio_guide"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "classroom_workstation",
              "type": "predicate"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "next_step1"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step2_base_pairing",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Complementary Base Pairing Rules and Hydrogen Bonding",
          "description": "DNA stability depends on specific base-pairing interactions. Adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T) via two hydrogen bonds; guanine (G) pairs with cytosine (C) via three hydrogen bonds. These rules are absolute and determine the sequence of one strand given the other. Examine the base pair models to see the geometry and bonding pattern. Why do these specific pairings occur? A-T pairing involves two hydrogen bonds because of the chemical structure of the bases; G-C pairing requires three bonds to achieve thermodynamic stability. This selective pairing underpins both DNA replication fidelity and the structure of the double helix."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "base_pair_models",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_helix_structure",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "base_pair_models",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "base_pairing_annotation",
              "poi": "adenine_thymine_pair"
            },
            {
              "target": "base_pair_models",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "base_pairing_annotation",
              "poi": "guanine_cytosine_pair"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "dna_double_helix",
              "type": "predicate"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "next_step2"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step3_antiparallel_orientation",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Antiparallel Strand Orientation and 5' to 3' Directionality",
          "description": "The two strands of DNA run in opposite directions\u2014antiparallel\u2014due to the chemistry of the sugar-phosphate backbone. Each backbone has directionality: one strand runs 5' to 3' (reading from the 5-carbon of the deoxyribose sugar to the 3-carbon) while the complementary strand runs 3' to 5'. This antiparallel orientation is not incidental; it is a direct consequence of how phosphodiester bonds form in the backbone. Examine the antiparallel strands diagram to visualize this arrangement. The 5'-to-3' directionality of each backbone is crucial: DNA polymerase can only synthesize DNA in the 5'-to-3' direction, which will later explain why the two strands replicate differently."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "dna_double_helix",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "antiparallel_strands_diagram",
              "poi": "sugar_phosphate_backbone_1"
            },
            {
              "target": "dna_double_helix",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "hydrogen_bonds_label",
              "poi": "sugar_phosphate_backbone_2"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "base_pair_models",
              "type": "predicate"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "next_step3"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step4_replication_fork_enzymes",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Replication Fork: Helicase, SSB Proteins, and Polymerase",
          "description": "DNA replication begins when helicase unwinds the double helix at the replication fork, breaking the hydrogen bonds between base pairs. Single-strand binding proteins (SSB proteins) then coat the exposed single strands, preventing them from re-annealing and secondary structure formation. DNA polymerase binds to each exposed template strand and synthesizes a new complementary strand by adding nucleotides in the 5'-to-3' direction. Watch the animations of each enzyme in action. Helicase unwinds; SSB proteins stabilize; polymerase adds nucleotides. This coordinated enzyme activity is the essence of DNA replication. Notice that the two template strands run in opposite directions\u2014this asymmetry will force different synthesis strategies for each new strand."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "replication_enzyme_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "helicase_unwinding_animation",
              "poi": "helicase_location"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_enzyme_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "single_strand_binding_proteins",
              "poi": "polymerase_location"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_enzyme_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "polymerase_synthesis_animation",
              "poi": "polymerase_location"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "dna_double_helix",
              "type": "predicate"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "next_step4"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step5_leading_lagging_asymmetry",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Leading and Lagging Strand Synthesis: Asymmetric Replication",
          "description": "Because DNA polymerase can only synthesize in the 5'-to-3' direction, and because the two template strands run antiparallel, the two new strands are synthesized differently. The leading strand is synthesized continuously in the 5'-to-3' direction, following the replication fork as helicase unwinds the helix. The lagging strand, however, runs 3'-to-5' relative to the replication fork's direction of movement. Polymerase cannot synthesize continuously in that direction; instead, it must synthesize the lagging strand discontinuously in short fragments called Okazaki fragments. Each Okazaki fragment is synthesized 5'-to-3' but is oriented \"backward\" relative to fork movement. DNA ligase subsequently joins these fragments together. This asymmetry\u2014continuous versus discontinuous synthesis\u2014is a direct consequence of the antiparallel structure and the unidirectional activity of DNA polymerase."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "leading_lagging_strand_diagram",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "okazaki_fragments_animation",
              "poi": "lagging_strand_area"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_ligase_joining_animation",
              "poi": "lagging_strand_area"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "replication_enzyme_set",
              "type": "predicate"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "next_step5"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step6_semiconservative_replication",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Semiconservative Replication: Parental Strands in Daughter Molecules",
          "description": "Why is DNA replication described as semiconservative? After one round of replication, each daughter DNA molecule contains one complete parental strand (either the original 5'-to-3' or the original 3'-to-5' strand) and one newly synthesized strand. Neither daughter molecule is entirely \"new\"\u2014each retains half of the original parental molecule. After a second round of replication, one granddaughter molecule will contain two original parental strands, and three molecules will contain one parental and one newly synthesized strand. This pattern was proven experimentally by Meselson and Stahl using isotopic labeling. The semiconservative mechanism ensures that genetic information is accurately propagated to daughter cells while allowing each strand to serve as a template for synthesis of its complement."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "classroom_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "semiconservative_replication_diagram",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_zone",
              "type": "predicate"
            }
          ]
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "classroom_workstation",
              "type": "predicate"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "end_activity"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
